+/// pokezero gen3 fidelity fix: CONFUSION is a BOUNDED stochastic duration, not a
+/// permanent volatile. Showdown rolls `this.effectState.time = this.random(2, 6)`
+/// once, at `addVolatile` (data/conditions.ts `confusion.onStart`; gen3 inherits
+/// gen4 -> gen5 -> ... and no mod in that chain overrides `onStart`), so
+/// `time` is uniform on {2,3,4,5}. gen3's `onBeforeMove` is the gen4 mod's
+/// (data/mods/gen4/conditions.ts): it decrements FIRST, snaps out when the
+/// counter reaches zero and lets the move through, and only otherwise rolls the
+/// 50% self-hit. The mon therefore takes a self-hit roll on `time - 1` attacking
+/// turns — uniform on {1,2,3,4} — and never more than four.
+///
+/// `turns_confused` counts attacking turns on which the confusion check has
+/// already run (the `volatile_status_durations.confusion` counter, incremented
+/// in `generate_instructions_from_existing_status_conditions`). Conditioned on
+/// having survived `n` such turns, the chance this was the last one is
+/// `1 / (1 + MAX_CONFUSION_TURNS - n)` — 1/4, 1/3, 1/2, 1 — the identical
+/// hazard ladder gen3 sleep already uses above, because gen3 sleep is rolled
+/// from the same `random(2, 6)` shape. The ladder is exact, not an
+/// approximation: it reproduces the uniform-on-{1,2,3,4} marginal exactly while
+/// keeping the per-turn self-hit chance at 50%, and it costs one extra branch
+/// per confused turn instead of the four-way fan-out a roll-at-application
+/// model would need.
+///
+/// Note on WHEN this fires: the ladder is evaluated at END OF TURN
+/// (`add_end_of_turn_branches`) rather than at the start of the next attacking
+/// turn. That is forced by the engine's single-surviving-branch structure — a
+/// snap-out branch has to CONTINUE through the move, and only one branch can,
+/// whereas at end of turn both branches are terminal. The two are equivalent for
+/// everything observable: Showdown's snap-out turn carries no self-hit roll and
+/// lets the move through, exactly like a turn on which the engine already
+/// dropped the volatile. The only residue is that Showdown keeps an inert
+/// `time == 1` confusion visible for one decision boundary longer, which the
+/// engine has already cleared; see docs/engine_fidelity_findings.md.
+fn chance_confusion_ends(turns_confused: i8) -> f32 {
+    if turns_confused <= 0 {
+        0.0
+    } else if turns_confused >= MAX_CONFUSION_TURNS {
+        1.0
+    } else {
+        1.0 / (1 + MAX_CONFUSION_TURNS - turns_confused) as f32
+    }
+}
